<p>Having grown up in the Mediterranean means having a few cooling staples like this cucumber, watermelon, and feta salad in your back pocket. Summers get so hot that refreshing, no-cook dishes like this are a staple on every table.</p>



<p>Versions of this salad show up all across the Mediterranean, from Italy to Greece and beyond. The ingredients may vary slightly, but the combination of juicy watermelon, crisp cucumbers, briny feta cheese, and fresh herbs is a classic in many Mediterranean kitchens.</p>



<p>Every year, as soon as watermelons showed up at the market, my mom would start making it on repeat. With the sweet juiciness of watermelon, the crunch of cold cucumbers, and the creamy-salty bite of feta, it&rsquo;s the kind of dish that doesn&rsquo;t need much fuss to shine.</p>



<p>This is what summer tastes like where I&rsquo;m from: easy, filling, and somehow always the first thing to disappear from the table.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-watermelon-feta-mint-salad-ingredients">Watermelon Feta Mint Salad Ingredients</h2>



<p>This summer watermelon salad with feta and cucumber starts with an easy honey-lime dressing and a short list of ingredients. You can find the complete list in the <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/minted-watermelon-salad-bulgur/#wprm-recipe-container-13320">recipe card</a> below, but here are a few helpful notes to consider when shopping for this refreshing salad.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="800" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/watermelon-salad-Ingredients.jpg" alt="The ingredients for watermelon cucumber salad from the top view." class="wp-image-82189" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/watermelon-salad-Ingredients.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/watermelon-salad-Ingredients-350x233.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/watermelon-salad-Ingredients-768x512.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/watermelon-salad-Ingredients-400x267.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/watermelon-salad-Ingredients-150x100.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/watermelon-salad-Ingredients.jpg"></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-for-the-salad-dressing">For the Salad Dressing:</h3>



<p>I use my <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/honey-lime-dressing/">honey-lime dressing</a> in this watermelon mint feta salad because the sweet, floral brightness of lime and honey pairs beautifully with the natural sweetness of the watermelon.</p>



<p><strong>Olive Oil: </strong>Choose a light and balanced extra virgin olive oil that isn&rsquo;t overly peppery or grassy. You want something that supports the salad without overpowering the other ingredients. My current favorite single-source olive oil brand is <a href="https://amzn.to/45eURLl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener nofollow">Garcia de la Cruz</a> (affiliate link.)</p>



<p><strong>Lime Juice:</strong> Freshly squeezed lime juice is best, but bottled works in a pinch. If you don&rsquo;t have lime, freshly squeezed lemon juice is an acceptable substitute. It will taste slightly different but still refreshing. You can also add a little lime zest (about &frac14; teaspoon) to enhance the citrus notes. Just be sure to zest the lime before juicing it.</p>



<p><strong>Honey: </strong>A floral local honey works especially well here, but maple syrup or agave are good alternatives if that&rsquo;s what you have. Just know they&rsquo;ll shift the flavor slightly.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-for-the-salad">For the Salad:</h3>



<p><strong>Watermelon:</strong> You&rsquo;ll need 8 cups of watermelon cut into small 1-inch cubes. Seedless watermelon is the easiest option, but if you&rsquo;re using regular watermelon, try cutting along the natural lines of the seeds to remove them more easily.</p>



<p><strong>Cucumbers:</strong> Persian cucumbers offer great crunch, but regular English cucumbers also work well. I usually peel mine, especially if the skin tastes bitter, which can happen if the fruit has been sitting for a while. Removing the seeds is optional.</p>



<p><strong>Fresh Herbs:</strong> The classic combo is watermelon, feta, and mint, but I like to add a little torn fresh basil for even more freshness. I don&rsquo;t recommend using dried herbs here, as their flavors tend to overpower the dish.</p>



<p><strong>Feta Cheese:</strong> I recommend buying a block of feta and crumbling it yourself. It&rsquo;s creamier, fresher, and gives you more control over the size and texture of the crumbles. If you&rsquo;re looking for alternatives, mild cheeses like goat cheese or queso fresco would also work well.</p>
</div>



